 fetched head, pushes with a lease, and retries up to 3 times (re-applying the whole-file snapshot is the rebase; if upstream changed one of its paths the request fails with a conflict instead of overwriting). A replay of an unchanged tree is a no-op that returns the upstream head. It refuses any change outside the `.ard/<issue#>/` directories. Worker code takes a separate lane: `Branches.CommitCode` commits only paths `committer.CheckCodePath` accepts (nothing under `.ard/`, `.ard-input/` or `.github/`, no `.git` or secret-shaped names), only to `worker/` branches, creating a branch with a push whose lease expects it to be absent and refusing to overwrite a path that changed on the branch since the job started. The one other lane is `ard init --pr`'s (local mode writes only the working tree, #303): it commits only `.ard/config.yaml`, `.ard/ard-init.lock` and files under the four role trees, only to the new branch `ard-init` cut from `main`, with a push whose lease expects the ref to be absent, so it creates the branch and never updates an existing one, and opens a PR; a human merges it with a squash or merge commit, so the bot-commit check never sees a bot commit outside `.ard/<issue#>/` on main's first-parent history. Because the bot must bypass branch protection on `main`, a detective check audits it: the workflow `.github/workflows/bot-commits.yml` walks the first-parent history of `main` (the last 500 commits, or everything after the repository variable `ARD_BOT_CHECK_SINCE`) and fails the run, listing the commits and paths in the job summary, when a commit made by the bot changes anything but regular files under a single `.ard/<issue#>/` directory. Bot commits are identified by **committer** (the App's noreply email or login), not author, because a squash merge of a bot PR has the bot as author. If the 500-commit window is full and its oldest commit is the bot's, the check fails closed until `ARD_BOT_CHECK_SINCE` is set, so a bad commit cannot be flushed out of view. It runs hourly as well as on push, because `[skip ci]` suppresses the push trigger. It fails closed when the `ARD_BOT_LOGIN` repository variable is unset. It reads both variables from repository settings and lives in `.github/workflows/`, which the App cannot write. Alerting is the failed run only; it does not open issues or pause ard.\ndocs/proposal.md:306:Local-executor details: the role directory (and its `Dockerfile`) is mounted from the config snapshot at the job's SHA, not from the working tree. The job worktree is always detached at a SHA resolved from the job's ref (the executor never checks out a branch; the committer owns branch worktrees). The controller's clone is a bare repository ard owns at `~/.ard/repos/<owner>/<repo>.git` (lowercased), with no remote, no stored credential and auto-gc off (`gc.auto=0`, `maintenance.auto=false`). `ard serve` creates it on first start and seeds it with one fetch of `main` (up to 30 min; if that fails, serve starts paused and the next start retries), and later starts reuse it. Config loads and committer lanes fetch into it, and every committer and executor worktree is cut from it (lanes under `~/.ard/work/<owner>/<repo>/lanes/`). `ard serve` never writes to the operator's checkout: it reads it only for the `origin` fallback of `--repo` and the local `paused` key. To rebuild a damaged clone, stop serve, `rm -rf` it and restart. Upgrading from a version that used the checkout: ard deletes its old lane and job worktree directories without running git there, so the old `refs/ard/*` refs and the dangling worktree registrations stay in the checkout until you remove them (`git for-each-ref --format='delete %(refname)' refs/ard/ | git update-ref --stdin; git worktree prune`). Every `git worktree add`, `remove` and `prune` on the controller's clone, by the executor and the committer's lanes alike, takes one in-process lock keyed by the clone's common git directory, because git's worktree bookkeeping is not safe under concurrent admin commands; fetches, commits and pushes do not take it. Each such command also has a 30 s deadline, and cleanup runs even when the job's context is cancelled, so one hung git cannot stall every lane. Do not run `git worktree prune` (or add/remove) in `~/.ard/repos/<owner>/<repo>.git` by hand while `ard serve` runs: the lock is per process and cannot cover a human's git. The operator's own checkout is free to use. The host keeps two files per job: `<base>.log` (stdout and stderr combined, the heartbeat source and the committed log) and `<base>.stdout` (stdout only, parsed by the adapter). A read-only job (`ReadOnly`, used by scanners) gets the worktree mounted `:ro` under Docker, or `chmod -R a-w` on the host. The heartbeat is the log file's mtime while the PID is alive; liveness alone is not a beat. Phase 0 local jobs do not survive a controller restart: the pipes belong to the controller, so after a restart the job reports `orphaned` (PID alive, start time verified) or `gone`, and the Reaper kills and fails it. The executor is not an effect: it never calls GitHub or commits, so jobs run normally under `--dry-run`. Sidecar auth: the sidecar fetch runs in the controller with the GitHub App installation token in a host-scoped `http.<url>.extraheader` passed through git's environment, never argv; the job receives plain files and no credential, and the cache stores no remote and no token. The App must be installed on the sidecar repo with contents:read. Under `executor: k8s` the `pre` container instead gets a per-job installation token scoped to contents:read on the job's repositories (`github.AppAuth.MintReadToken`), revoked when the job ends; a personal access token cannot be scoped down, so that executor needs GitHub App auth. Host-mode jobs can reach `~/.ard/sidecars`, but it holds no credential. Host-mode jobs run as the controller's uid: they can read the GitHub App key and `~/.config/gh` by absolute path, write the controller's clone, and undo `chmod a-w`. Host mode is allowed for every role ([#84](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/84)), but a role that reads untrusted input (every triage, worker and reviewer role, and an LLM scanner) warns loudly when it has no `Dockerfile`; deterministic scanners are exempt, and nothing warns under `executor: k8s`, where every job runs in a container. `ard config check` and the first successful config load of `ard serve` (and any role a later config adds) log one `executor.host_untrusted` WARN per such role and also write it to stderr, so it survives `--log-level`. The local executor logs the WARN again for every host-mode job of such a role and appends a `host_untrusted` row to the `events` table. **Default agent image under the local executor (#244, part of #55).** `ard serve` and `ard config check` read the host setting `ARD_AGENT_IMAGE`: a digest-pinned reference (`<name>@sha256:<64 hex>`) or a local image ID (`sha256:<hex>` from `make agent-image`); tags are refused (`executor.CheckImageRef`, `ErrImageRef`), and an invalid value stops startup. When it is set, `Prepare` runs every job that reads untrusted input and has no `Dockerfile` (triage, worker agent, test and fix steps, reviewer, and LLM scanner jobs) under Docker in that image, with the same flags and mounts as a Dockerfile role (`--cap-drop ALL`, `no-new-privileges`, worktree at `/work`, role directory `:ro`, and so on). A role's own `Dockerfile` wins. Deterministic scanners (trusted input) stay on the host. A local image is used as is; a digest that is not present is pulled. There is no fallback to host mode: `ard serve` runs `docker version` at startup and fails if Docker is down, and a job whose Docker setup fails at `Prepare` fails there. The controller's clone is not mounted, so `git` has no repository inside the container; every Docker job gets `GOFLAGS=-buildvcs=false` so `go build` does not fail on VCS stamping. Unset keeps today's behaviour. With it set, `ard config check` and `ard serve` no longer log or record `executor.host_untrusted` for roles it covers, and the hint of the warning names `ARD_AGENT_IMAGE`. Each warning lists what the job can reach, including `~/.ard` (state, config snapshots, the pause file, other jobs' worktrees and logs). The owner accepts this risk while ard runs only on owner-controlled repos, and revisits it when moving to the spark cluster, where every job runs in a container once the k8s executor (#54) lands. Until then the Gate 1 dry-run soak on spark (see Rollout and Delivery) runs `ard serve` with the local executor in a pod, and the deterministic cve scanner runs there as a host subprocess of the controller container: its role directory has no `Dockerfile`, and the `ard` image carries its pinned tools. Only deterministic scanners run in that pod: it starts `ard serve --loops scanners,stale,reaper` ([#187](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/187)), so the triage, worker and reviewer loops never run there, even though `.ard/config.yaml` on `main` has agent roles. `--loops` takes a comma-separated list of loop names (scanners, stale, triage, workers, reviewers, reviewfix, merger, janitor, reaper, budget; `budget` is the status-issue notice loop, the claim gate is always on), defaults to every loop, and rejects an unknown name with exit 2. `ard serve` and `ard config check` refuse to run on git older than 2.47.0, because the committer's fetches fail on older versions.\ndocs/proposal.md:356:5. **Guard the diff** after each agent or fix step, before anything leaves the host. The worktree belongs to the agent, so the guard never trusts its `.git`, config, index or attributes: git runs against the controller's own clone with a private index filled from the base commit (so `skip-worktree` and `assume-unchanged` hide nothing, and no planted filter, textconv, fsmonitor or hook runs), with its objects in a private temp directory (the clone is only read, so an agent cannot grow it); a worktree with a new or resized file over 1 MiB, or more than 256 of them, is held before anything is hashed. The scanned bytes are copied to a controller-private directory and the code lane commits the copies, so nothing can change a file between the scan and the commit. Close-out's changed-file listing uses the same hardened path. Paths under `.ard/<issue#>/` and `.ard-input/` belong to close-out and the loop and are left out. Everything else is checked, and any rejection holds the job for a human (`ard:needs-human`, nothing pushed): the path rule in `committer.CheckCodePath` (all of `.github/`, the rest of `.ard/`, `.git` segments, `.gitmodules`, any `.gitattributes` (`linguist-generated` or `-diff` would collapse files in the GitHub PR diff, so a worker change to one always goes to a human), secret-shaped names), any symlink, nested repository or other non-regular file, a file over 1 MiB, a binary file, and more than 256 changed files. The credential scan runs twice: the content of every written file is checked for the values of known secrets (the hold names the variable, never the value), and the built-in pattern scanner runs over it with only the findings on lines this job added kept, so a worker that edits a file that already holds a credential-shaped fixture is not held for lines it did not write. The comment names file, line and rule (`internal/foo_test.go:12: secret scan rule aws-access-key`), never the text. A scanner error fails closed. Test fixtures that look like credentials must be built at run time, as `internal/redact`'s tests do (`\"AK\" + \"IA\" + ...`); a literal is held, and there is no in-band escape. The changed lines of the whole PR (the diff against the merge base with `main`) are checked against two per-worker-role thresholds. Over `diff_max_lines` (default 3,000) also holds, as `diff guard: N changed lines, limit M`. Over `diff_warn_lines` (default 2,000) but not over the limit, the PR goes ahead: the loop logs `worker.diff_large` (lines, warn, max), and the PR body and the close-out issue comment carry a fixed note, `Large change: N changed lines (warn at W)`, so reviewers know; the note quotes nothing from the change. The latest step's count decides, so a fix round that shrinks the PR drops the note. The comment shows a rejection reason as `diff guard: <entry>; <entry>; <entry>; and N more`.\ndocs/proposal.md:357:6. **Two commits per round.** The loop commits the code through the committer's code lane (`Branches.CommitCode`) with the title `[<type>: ]<issue title> (#<issue>)`, the redacted summary (secrets and host paths) as the body and an `Ard-Job: <job-id>` trailer, no `[skip ci]`. `<type>` comes from the issue's `type:*` label (`feature` is `feat`, `bug` is `fix`; `docs`, `chore`, `refactor` and `test` keep their names) and with no `type:*` label the `<type>: ` part is dropped and the title is `<issue title> (#<issue>)`. The prefix is never taken from the plan or the lead's routing, so it depends on a maintainer applying `type:*` at intake. At the end close-out commits `worker-<role>.md` and the redacted logs of every agent step to the same branch (or to `main`, as triage's are, when the job stopped before its first push). `.ard/<issue#>/` never goes through the code lane. A push failure (branch exists, conflict, branch gone, guard) fails the job with no automatic retry.\ndocs/proposal.md:510:**`ard init` creates and upgrades `.ard/` (decided, #27; revised, #303).** It builds `config.yaml` and the starter roles in memory (today `scanners/cve`, `scanners/lts`, `scanners/docs`, `scanners/error-codes`, `scanners/lint`, `scanners/missing-tests`, `scanners/trufflehog`, `scanners/trufflehog-history`, `triage/lead`, `triage/engineer`, `workers/engineer`, `reviewers/eng-review`, `reviewers/license-review`, `reviewers/product-manager-review` and `reviewers/security-review`; `intake: {mode: label}`, the protected-path defaults above, and the cve, lts, docs, error-codes, lint, lead, engineer, worker, eng-review, security-review, license-review and product-manager-review overrides), then brings `.ard/` up to them. By default it writes the **local working tree** for a human to commit; with `--pr` it commits them on a new branch `ard-init` cut from `main` through the effect layer and a narrow committer lane, pushes it and opens a PR, as before. Either way a human commits or merges: ard never writes `main`, and `scan` and `aid` are written `100755`. **Upgrades** are tracked in `.ard/ard-init.lock`, a YAML file holding the sha256 of every file init created or updated; init adds or refreshes a file's entry whenever it writes that file. The user may edit the lock; ard uses it only to decide whether a file changed since init wrote it. Per file: absent, create it; identical to the new starter, leave it; hash equal to its lock entry, update it; anything else (including no lock or no entry) counts as edited and is never overwritten: init lists it with the starter's diff. A file the starter dropped is listed, never deleted, and init writes nothing outside `.ard/`. The three repo context templates (`.ard/PRODUCT.md`, `.ard/VISION.md`, `.ard/TECH_STACK.md`) are create-if-absent: written only when missing, never treated as an upgrade and never overwritten; the next steps tell an adopter with an existing config to add their paths to `merge.protected_paths`. In local mode init refuses while `.ard/` has uncommitted changes; in `--pr` mode it refuses while `ard-init` exists on the remote, whatever its PR's state, so it never force-pushes over or stacks commits on a human's edits. If nothing differs it prints `nothing to change`. Init runs the shared permission check (#302: repo reachable, App installed, the permissions ard needs) and reports it; a failed check warns in local mode and fails in `--pr` mode. It no longer syncs labels: that moved to `ard setup`. With `--dry-run` it prints the plan and writes nothing (local mode) or logs the commit, push and PR to the ledger (`--pr`). It ends by naming the next steps: commit, merge with **Squash and merge** or **Create a merge commit** (never a fast-forward from the command line, which would put the bot's commit on main's first-parent history in `--pr` mode; **Rebase and merge** is also safe, #85), then `ard setup`. When the lint scanner is among the files, it also says the scanner fails until `.ard/scanners/lint/baseline` is committed.\ndocs/proposal.md:532:- **Role files are human-owned.** The diff guard and the committer's code lane reject any worker change under `.ard/` or `.github/` (so `.ard/config.yaml`, `.ard/PRODUCT.md`, `.ard/VISION.md`, `.ard/TECH_STACK.md` and every role directory), and the merger treats those paths as protected through `merge.protected_paths` (the `ard init` defaults), so no agent can rewrite its own prompt. `ard init` only proposes them, in a PR a human merges.\ndocs/proposal.md:571:- **Versioning ([#183](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/183)).** `version.yml` gives every first-parent merge to `main` an annotated tag `vX.Y.Z` and a notes-only release, oldest first. The bump comes from the merged PR's labels: `semver:major`, `semver:minor`, else patch; a direct push gets patch, and ard's own main-lane commits (committer `ARD_BOT_LOGIN`) are skipped. The seed tag is `v0.1.0`. The workflow is a dry run until the repository variable `ARD_VERSIONING` is `on`. `make build` (`git describe` limited to plain `vX.Y.Z` tags) and `go install ...@vX.Y.Z` (build info) both print the tag from `ard version`. `internal/versioncheck` tests the workflow's scripts. See `docs/runbooks/release.md`.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01JvLQQwZXn6faq3GswXNbeh","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
